## The essay text for `key`, or "".
pub func essay_text(key: str) -> str:
    match key:
        "look/2001-a-space-odyssey-1968":
            return "Kubrick and Geoffrey Unsworth shot 2001 in Super Panavision 70 on 65 mm Eastman Color Negative, very likely 5251; its faster successor 5254 was only presented in May 1968, after the premiere. The 70 mm prints came from MGM's Metrocolor lab; 35 mm prints were Technicolor dye-transfer reductions. The Dawn of Man was shot on a stage with front projection, and the Star Gate with slit-scan.\n\nThis look exposes 5251 on 65 mm, develops it in ECN-1, prints it on Vision 2383 standing in for the period print film, and projects it."
        "look/barry-lyndon-1975":
            return "Kubrick and John Alcott pushed the whole film a stop on 5254, so interiors and candlelit scenes matched. The candlelight was possible only with a Zeiss 50 mm f/0.7 lens built for NASA. On exteriors Alcott never used an 85 filter on the tungsten stock and took out the blue in printing, and a Tiffen Low Contrast No. 3 softened every shot.\n\nThis look uses 5254 pushed in ECN-1 (16 minutes, an inference) and prints it balanced, as the timer would have. The Low Contrast filter is not modelled."
        "look/days-of-heaven-1978":
            return "Néstor Almendros shot most of it at the magic hour, with no fill light. As the light went he pushed the negative a stop, then pulled the 85 filter to gain nearly a stop more, and finally slowed the camera. Nights were pushed one or two stops. The prints were blown up to 70 mm.\n\nAlmendros shot Kodak's 5247, the first ECN-2 negative, which is not here; EXR 200T 5293 stands in. This look pushes it a stop and takes off the 85, so the light falls on the film as it was."
        "look/fight-club-1999":
            return "Jeff Cronenweth rated his stocks at box speed and exposed generously, so the negatives were dense and printed down. Vision 500T 5279 carried the interiors and nights, some night exteriors were flashed about five percent in the lab, and a number of prints went through Technicolor's ENR at an infrared level of 0.80.\n\nThis look prints 5279 from Super 35 with silver kept at 0.80. The daylight stocks, EXR 5248 and Vision 250D 5246, are not modelled; for the flashed night exteriors, add 5 percent Flashing on the Film stage."
        "look/friends-seasons-1-5":
            return "Friends was shot on 35 mm Panavision negative under the bright, warm light of a multi-camera sitcom stage, developed and transferred on a telecine to standard-definition tape, never printed. No source names its stocks; a fast tungsten Kodak negative of the EXR line is the likely choice for the early years.\n\nThis look uses EXR 500T 5298 through the television chain, its telecine timing warm and a little magenta, flatter and grainier, matched to the cast of the broadcast's own frames."
        "look/friends-seasons-6-10":
            return "The later seasons look brighter and cleaner, consistent with Kodak's Vision stocks, which replaced EXR from 1996, and with changes in the show's telecine work.\n\nThis look uses Vision 500T 5279 through the television chain, timed warmer and less magenta than the early seasons and with less grain, matched to the broadcast's frames. As for the early seasons, the stock is an inference: no source names it."
        "look/lawrence-of-arabia-1962":
            return "Freddie Young photographed the desert in Super Panavision 70 on 65 mm Eastman Color negative, for 70 mm prints. The mirage was shot on a Panavision telephoto of over 400 mm. \"I just shot outdoors and stopped down,\" Young said.\n\nThe negative was most likely 5250, which 5251 replaced at the same speed in 1962; 5251 stands in. This look exposes it on 65 mm through an 85 filter, as a tungsten stock in daylight needs, develops it in ECN-1, prints and projects it. Period print stock is not modelled: Vision 2383 stands in."
        "look/moonrise-kingdom-2012":
            return "Robert Yeoman shot every frame on Kodak Vision3 200T in Super 16, the 500-speed stocks being too grainy for Wes Anderson, with no filtration, not even an 85 in daylight. The palettes are the production's design; in the grade, Yeoman says, Anderson takes the look a bit warmer and pushes the saturation.\n\nThis look uses Vision3 200T on 16 mm through the scan and grade, warmer and more saturated. The amounts are an inference from his account."
        "look/munich-2005-bypassed-negative":
            return "For the semi-documentary scenes, the refugee camps and the Munich assault, the bleach was skipped on the camera negative itself, at Technicolor in Los Angeles and London and at Éclair in Paris. Silver left on a negative sits in the scene's highlights, so whites blow out. To tame the contrast the negative was pulled one and a half stops: \"It's almost like shooting color reversal,\" Kamiński said. The prints then also had ENR, so the film stacks both.\n\nThis look keeps all the negative's silver, develops it for about 2:10 instead of 3:00 (Kodak tables a one-stop pull at 2:30; the extra half stop is extrapolated), and prints with ENR at 0.70."
        "look/munich-2005":
            return "Janusz Kamiński shot Munich in Super 35 on Kodak Vision 500T 5279, EXR 200T 5293 and Fuji negatives, and every release print went through Technicolor's ENR at an infrared level of 65 to 75. ENR adds a black-and-white development bath that leaves silver in the print where it is dense, so blacks turn rich and colors drain. It was not the first film to keep silver: Kamiński had used ENR on Amistad and Saving Private Ryan, and bleach bypass goes back to 1960.\n\nThis look prints 5279 on Vision 2383 with its silver kept at an infrared density of 0.70. The Fuji stocks of the Middle East scenes are not modelled."
        "look/nineteen-eighty-four-1984":
            return "Roger Deakins had the bleach bypassed on all the prints, at Kays Laboratory in London, rather than on the camera negative. Earlier home releases did not show it; Deakins has said only the recent Criterion release reflects the intent. Darius Khondji named it as an inspiration for Delicatessen.\n\nThis look prints with all the silver kept, an infrared density of 2.40. The camera negative is not documented: EXR 200T stands in."
        "look/o-brother-where-art-thou-2000":
            return "The Coens and Roger Deakins wanted the faded look of hand-tinted postcards: dry, golden fields. Bleach bypass and Deluxe's ACE were tested and rejected because they drain all colors alike. Instead the whole film was scanned at 2K, and for ten weeks colorist Julius Friede at Cinesite turned the greens of grass and trees toward dry yellows and browns, then recorded it back to film. It was the first feature finished entirely through a digital intermediate.\n\nThis look runs Vision 500T 5279 from Super 35 through the scan and grade, turning greens toward yellow and lowering their saturation. The amounts are an inference from the accounts."
        "look/oppenheimer-2023-black-and-white":
            return "For Strauss's timeline Kodak made Double-X 5222 in 65 mm for the first time, prototype cans with handwritten labels. FotoKem had never processed 65 mm black and white, and refilled a 65 mm processor with black-and-white chemistry for it. The cameras needed new gates for the stock's thickness.\n\nThis look uses Double-X on 65 mm, developed and scanned. Its rating, developer and print stock were not published."
        "look/oppenheimer-2023-color":
            return "Hoyte van Hoytema shot on 65 mm, IMAX 15-perforation and Panavision System 65: Vision3 250D for exteriors and bright interiors, 500T for low light. The color scenes are Oppenheimer's own point of view. FotoKem printed everything, and IMAX 70 mm, 70 mm and 35 mm film prints went out, with a digital master matched to them.\n\nThis look uses Vision3 250D on 65 mm, printed and projected. The IMAX frame, larger still, is not modelled."
        "look/saving-private-ryan-1998":
            return "Kamiński shot EXR 200T 5293 pushed a stop to 400, with a half Coral filter where an 85 would normally go, for a slightly blue cast. A Panaflasher flashed the negative about 15 percent, so the blacks would not be sharp and the color would desaturate, and the prints went through ENR, which the lab ran at an infrared level of 1.00, \"one of the highest levels of ENR used to date.\" Panavision stripped the coatings off his lenses for flare and flat contrast, and a 45 or 90 degree shutter froze every grain of sand.\n\nThis look sets all of that except the uncoated lenses and the shutter, which are not modelled. The half Coral's strength is an estimate."
        "look/se7en-1995":
            return "Darius Khondji wanted color noir, blacks as deep as black and white could give. Interiors were shot on EXR 200T 5293 at box speed, in Super 35, nearly wide open. A number of the release prints went through Deluxe's new CCE process, which keeps about three quarters of the print's silver: \"to get those beautiful blacks, it would drain out a lot of the color.\" Color drained in the lab was put back by the art department.\n\nThis look prints 5293 with its silver kept at an infrared density of 1.85, CCE's level. The film's other stocks, 5245 for exteriors and 5287 pushed a stop for nights, are not modelled."
        "look/the-french-dispatch-2021-black-and-white":
            return "About half of The French Dispatch is in black and white, shot on real black-and-white negative, Kodak's Eastman Double-X 5222, not desaturated color. Robert Yeoman had both stocks processed normally, \"without any push or pull,\" in Paris, in Super 35.\n\nThis look uses Double-X on Super 35, developed normally and scanned."
        "look/the-godfather-1972":
            return "Gordon Willis rated Eastman Color Negative at 250 and had it pushed a stop, \"theoretically a half-stop underexposure,\" lit from overhead and printed on one light for the whole film, with what he called a brassy yellow. The release prints were Technicolor dye transfer, and Sicily was shot through chocolate filters.\n\nThis look uses Eastman Color Negative 5254, half a stop under and pushed in ECN-1 (Kodak never tabled pushing it; 16 minutes is an inference), printed warm and yellow. The printer lights are an inference from Willis's words, and dye-transfer printing and chocolate filters are not modelled."
        "look/three-kings-1999-cross-processed":
            return "Once the characters leave the base, Sigel shot Ektachrome that Kodak made for the production and cross-processed it in negative chemistry: the crew signed a waiver, a small lab ran it, and \"off a half a stop, you were shit out of luck.\"\n\nThis look exposes Ektachrome E100, the nearest Ektachrome here, develops it in C-41 as a negative and prints it: harsh contrast, a clear base with no mask, and loud, shifted color."
        "look/three-kings-1999-the-base":
            return "For the opening on the American base, Newton Thomas Sigel skip-bleached the camera negative, \"not in the print, but in the negative, which is much more radical.\" David O. Russell's reference was newspaper color photos of the Gulf War, \"very contrasty,\" that \"blow out.\"\n\nThe stock was a Kodak daylight negative, most likely Vision 250D 5246; Vision3 250D stands in. This look keeps all the negative's silver and prints it."
        "mode/black-white":
            return "Black-and-white negative, developed in a developer of your choice and scanned. A color filter on the lens decides which colors become light or dark grey, and the developer decides speed, grain, sharpness and how the highlights are held.\n\nThis mode puts a Filter stage before the film for that reason, and starts from Fujifilm Acros II in D-76. Try Tri-X in Rodinal for sharp, gritty grain, or a red filter for dark skies."
        "mode/cinema":
            return "The classic chain of feature films until the digital intermediate took over in the 2000s. The camera negative was developed, timed with printer lights, printed onto a high-contrast print film and projected in a dark theater.\n\nHere the negative is printed on Kodak Vision 2383 and projected under a xenon arc. The print's own curve and dyes are the tone scale: deep blacks, saturated midtones, highlights that roll off. Printer lights time the color, and the print's silver can be kept, as Technicolor's ENR and Deluxe's CCE did."
        "mode/slide":
            return "Reversal film becomes the picture itself: developed in E-6, it is a positive, seen on a light table or projected. There is no negative, no print and no scanner's correction, so what the film does to color and contrast is what you see.\n\nThis mode exposes a slide stock, develops it, and shows it on a D50 lightbox. Slides have little latitude: expose carefully, and expect their saturated, contrasty character."
        "mode/super-panavision-70":
            return "A large-format process of the late 1950s and 1960s: spherical lenses on 65 mm negative running five perforations a frame, a picture 52.48 by 23.01 mm, nearly three and a half times the area of a 35 mm frame. Release prints were 70 mm, with room for six magnetic sound tracks. Lawrence of Arabia and 2001: A Space Odyssey were shot this way.\n\nThis mode exposes Eastman Color Negative 5251 on 65 mm, develops it in the period's ECN-1, prints and projects it. The large frame means the grain and softness of the film are small against the picture."
        "mode/television":
            return "From the 1950s into the 2000s most American television drama and comedy was shot on 35 mm negative and never printed. The negative was developed, then run through a telecine, a scanner that turned it straight into video while a colorist set its color scene by scene.\n\nThis mode is that chain: the camera's light, the film and its development, the scan, the colorist's grade, a display rendering, and the framing. It is the default because it is also how film is mostly seen today: scanned."
        "process/bleach-bypass":
            return "In a normal color process, the bleach turns all the developed silver back into halide and the fixer washes it away, leaving only the dyes. Skip the bleach, or weaken it, and a black-and-white silver image stays on top of the color one. Kodak puts it simply: the retained silver increases contrast and decreases color saturation.\n\nThe cinematographer Kazuo Miyagawa, who had trained as a chemist, invented it for Kon Ichikawa's Her Brother in 1960. Labs later made it controllable: Technicolor's ENR, named for Ernesto Novelli Rimo, who devised it for Vittorio Storaro on Reds, and Deluxe's CCE, which left about three quarters of the silver in the prints of Seven. Saving Private Ryan used one of the strongest ENR levels Technicolor had run, to drain color from faces.\n\nDone to the negative, as here, the silver sits where the negative is dense: in the scene's highlights. The lab's scan, set for a bleached negative, reads it as extra exposure, so highlights crowd together and colors fade. Labs advise underexposing about a stop. The silver can be bleached later, so a bypassed negative is not lost."
        "process/c-41":
            return "C-41 is the color negative process of still photography, introduced by Kodak in 1972 together with Kodacolor II. It runs at 37.8 °C (100 °F) with a developer time of three minutes fifteen seconds, and every minilab in the world could run it, which made color negative film the film of family snapshots for decades.\n\nIts developing agent, CD-4, is more active than ECN-2's. Still films are made for it, and need no rem-jet removal. Motion picture film run through C-41, as some labs do, comes out denser and more contrasty than in ECN-2, and its rem-jet has to be dealt with.\n\nKodak's push rule for C-41 is thirty seconds more per stop: 3:45 for one stop, 4:15 for two. A push raises contrast, the blue layer's most, and grain, but adds little real shadow speed: the shadows of an underexposed negative stay thin."
        "process/cross-processing":
            return "Develop slide film in C-41 and there is no first developer, no reversal: the color developer forms dye wherever the film was exposed, so the slide comes out a negative. It has none of a negative's orange mask, the colored couplers that correct the dyes' unwanted absorptions, and the reversal emulsion was made to be steep. Scanned or printed, the result is very contrasty and loudly colored, with crossovers: one hue in the highlights and its complement in the shadows. Fujifilm slide films are known to go yellow-green and cyan.\n\nIt was found by accident many times, and became a look of 1990s fashion and music photography. In cinema it appears in Clockers, U-Turn, and the \"Demon-Vision\" sequences of Fallen; Newton Thomas Sigel used cross-processed Ektachrome on Three Kings. Labs were wary of it: without E-6's final stabilizer, the magenta dye of cross-processed Ektachrome fades.\n\nHere the slide's curves are turned into the negative C-41 makes of them, at a lower contrast than the slide's own, over a clear base, and scanned like any negative, without the color correction a stock's own scan gets."
        "process/d-76-1-1":
            return "D-76 diluted with an equal part of water and thrown away after one use. Diluted, the sulfite that dissolves grain edges is weaker and the developer runs out sooner in dense areas, so Kodak notes \"greater sharpness\" and a \"slight increase in graininess\" against the stock solution.\n\nDevelopment takes longer, about ten and a half minutes for Acros II at 20 °C, and is less sensitive to temperature: Kodak's tables for diluted developers change about 5.5 % per degree, against 8 to 9 % for stock solutions. Many photographers use it as their everyday developer for its sharpness and its fresh, consistent results."
        "process/d-76":
            return "Kodak published D-76 in 1927, originally for motion picture negative film. It is a metol–hydroquinone developer with a large amount of sodium sulfite, about 100 grams per litre, which preserves it and also dissolves a little of the silver halide as it develops, softening the grain.\n\nD-76 became the reference developer of black-and-white photography: film speeds and grain are judged against it, and almost every data sheet gives its times. It gives full film speed, moderate grain and good sharpness, a balance few developers improve on in every respect at once. Ilford's ID-11 is essentially the same formula.\n\nDiluted 1:1 and used once, it gives slightly sharper negatives. Time and temperature go together: the Acros II data sheet gives 8½ minutes at 18 °C and 4½ at 26 °C."
        "process/diafine":
            return "Diafine splits the developer in two. In the first bath the film soaks up developing agents, which cannot work without alkali; in the second, the alkali switches them on. The film develops only with what its emulsion absorbed, so where the image is dense the developer runs out, while the shadows develop fully.\n\nThat has two remarkable consequences. Time and temperature hardly matter: the maker says they have no practical effect between 21 and 29 °C. And films come out faster at a moderate, fixed contrast: the maker rates Tri-X at EI 1600, Acros at 200. Independent tests usually find about one stop rather than two for Tri-X, with highlights that never block up."
        "process/e-6":
            return "E-6 makes a positive slide in one film. The first developer is a black-and-white developer: it turns exposed grains into a silver negative image and uses them up. A reversal bath then fogs every grain that was not exposed (older processes used a flash of light). The color developer develops those grains, forming dyes where the scene was dark. Bleach and fix remove all the silver, and the dyes remain as a positive.\n\nEverything depends on the first developer, at 37.8 °C for six minutes. Developing it longer uses up more of the grains, so less dye forms and the slide gets lighter: that is how slides are pushed. Each stop of push costs some density in the blacks and shifts color slightly.\n\nE-6 replaced the older E-4 in the late 1970s and remains the process for every slide film still made, from Velvia to Ektachrome."
        "process/ecn-1":
            return "Before ECN-2, Eastman Color Negative was developed in ECN-1. Its color developer, SD-30, worked slowly and cool: about twelve minutes at 21 °C, where ECN-2 takes three minutes at 41 °C. Faster, hotter processes came in the 1970s as labs needed more throughput and Kodak's emulsions changed.\n\nThis was the process of the great 65 mm productions of the 1960s, among them 2001: A Space Odyssey, shot in Super Panavision 70 on Eastman Color Negative. Kodak's 1968 paper introducing the next negative, 5254, gives the ECN-1 sequence, and states that 5254's spectral sensitivities and dyes were the same as 5251's, the negative of that era."
        "process/ecn-2":
            return "ECN-2 is the process for motion picture color negative, used since the 1970s. The film first goes through a prebath that softens the rem-jet, the black anti-halation backing, which is then washed off. Then the color developer, CD-3, works for three minutes at 41.1 °C (106 °F): where it reduces exposed silver halide, its oxidized form couples with the dye couplers in each layer, building cyan, magenta and yellow dye around each developing grain.\n\nA stop bath ends development, a bleach turns the silver back into halide, and a fixer dissolves all the remaining halide away. What is left is the dye image and the orange mask, colored couplers that did not react and correct the dyes' unwanted absorptions.\n\nKodak's push times are 3:40 for one stop and 4:40 for two; a pull is 2:30. A push buys less than a third of a stop of real shadow speed for the first stop and about half a stop for two; the rest is contrast, grain, and a drift toward yellow highlights and blue shadows. The developer is held to a tenth of a degree, because a contrast mismatch between rolls would show from reel to reel."
        "process/hc-110-b":
            return "HC-110 is a syrupy concentrate, diluted just before use; dilution B (1+31) is the one Kodak's times are given for. Its developing agents sit in an organic solvent rather than water, so the concentrate keeps for years, which made it the developer of schools, newspapers and photographers who develop only now and then.\n\nDevelopment is quick, under four minutes for Tri-X at 20 °C, and gives full speed with a slightly coarser grain than D-76 and good sharpness. It is also more sensitive to temperature than most, close to ten percent per degree."
        "process/id-11":
            return "Ilford's ID-11 is essentially the same formula as Kodak's D-76: metol and hydroquinone with about 100 grams per litre of sodium sulfite. Ilford describes it as giving full emulsion speed with fine grain, and it has been the reference developer in Ilford's own data sheets for decades.\n\nIlford's rule for temperature is simple and generous: ten percent more time for each degree below 20 °C, ten percent less for each degree above. Acros II's data sheet gives it 6¾ minutes at 20 °C."
        "process/microfine":
            return "Fujifilm's own fine-grain developer, and the one its Acros data sheet measures granularity in: an RMS granularity of 7 for Acros II, among the finest figures of any black-and-white film. Unlike most fine-grain developers it costs Acros no speed: ten minutes at 20 °C at EI 100.\n\nFujifilm's table for it runs from twelve and a half minutes at 18 °C to under six at 26 °C, about ten percent per degree."
        "process/microphen":
            return "Microphen is Ilford's speed-increasing developer: phenidone and hydroquinone, buffered with borax at low alkalinity. Phenidone, discovered at Ilford by J. D. Kendall in 1940, is barely held back by the bromide that development releases, so development keeps going in the deepest shadows.\n\nIlford claims up to half a stop more speed than ID-11 with comparable grain, a high \"speed to grain ratio\". It is the developer Ilford recommends for pushing HP5 Plus, with times given up to EI 3200."
        "process/perceptol":
            return "Perceptol is a fine-grain developer of the solvent type, like Kodak's Microdol-X: metol with a large amount of sulfite and some chloride, at a nearly neutral pH. It dissolves a good part of each grain as it develops, and that silver is redeposited as fine particles, so the image is made of smaller specks.\n\nThe price is speed. Ilford rates HP5 Plus at EI 250 and Tri-X at EI 200 in Perceptol, against 400 in ID-11. Photographers use it when the finest grain matters more than speed: large prints from 35 mm, smooth skies, portraits."
        "process/pmk-pyro":
            return "Pyrogallol was one of the first developing agents, used from the 1850s. As it develops it oxidizes into products that stain and harden the gelatin around each grain, in proportion to the silver. Gordon Hutchings revived it in the 1980s as PMK (Pyro, Metol, Kodalk), described in The Book of Pyro, and it became a favourite of large-format photographers.\n\nThe stain does two things. It masks the grain, so prints look smoother than the silver alone would. And it is yellow-green, so it adds printing density in the highlights mainly for blue-sensitive papers, compressing them. The hardened gelatin also limits the sideways diffusion of developer, which gives sharp, well-separated edges."
        "process/rodinal-1-100-stand":
            return "A very dilute developer left almost untouched: the film is agitated at the start and then stands for an hour. Where the image is dense, the developer around it is exhausted within minutes and development stops; in the shadows it continues. The result is strong compensation, highlights held back and shadows built up, and strong edge effects as exhausted developer and released bromide spread around dense areas.\n\nAgfa's own figures for 1+100 show real speed loss, Tri-X at about EI 250. Stand development has risks: bromide-laden developer can stream down from dense areas, leaving streaks known as bromide drag. Photographers use it for scenes of extreme contrast and for its etched, glowing edges."
        "process/rodinal-1-25":
            return "Rodinal is one of the oldest photographic products still made: Momme Andresen patented it for Agfa in 1891. It is a concentrate of p-aminophenol in strong alkali with almost no sulfite, diluted just before use.\n\nWith little sulfite there is no solvent action on the grain. Each grain develops whole and sharp-edged, so the grain is crisp and clearly defined, and Agfa sold it for its \"very good contour sharpness\". It costs a little speed, about a third of a stop. Its look is the classic one of gritty, sharp black-and-white: every grain visible, every edge clear."
        "process/rodinal-1-50":
            return "Rodinal diluted twice as far. The weaker developer exhausts quickly in dense areas: highlights are held back while shadows keep developing, which compresses a contrasty scene, and fresh developer diffusing into a thin area beside a dense one makes edges stand out, the Eberhard effect.\n\nDevelopment takes about twelve minutes for Tri-X at 20 °C, rated around EI 400 at best. It is a favourite for its sharpness and tonality, with grain as defined as at 1+25."
        "process/t-max-developer":
            return "A liquid developer Kodak made for its T-MAX films, whose flat, tabular \"T-grain\" crystals capture light efficiently but need a developer that brings out their full speed. Kodak describes it as giving enhanced shadow detail, at normal ratings and when pushing.\n\nIt is used at 1+4 and gives roughly a third of a stop more shadow speed than D-76, with a little more grain. T-MAX P3200, a film meant to be pushed, was usually developed in it."
        "process/xtol-1-1":
            return "XTOL diluted with an equal part of water and used once. Kodak's sheet promises \"slightly greater film speed\" and \"enhanced sharpness\" than the stock solution, with slightly more grain: the usual trade of dilution, where a weaker developer exhausts locally at edges and brings out fine detail.\n\nLike other dilutions it is less sensitive to temperature, about 5.5 % per degree, which makes it forgiving in a home darkroom."
        "process/xtol":
            return "XTOL, introduced by Kodak in 1996, replaced the hydroquinone of classic developers with ascorbate, vitamin C, and uses a phenidone-type agent, Dimezone S. It is low in toxicity and was designed to give more from the same film.\n\nKodak claimed that most films gain about a third of a stop of real speed in XTOL, and that prints can be ten percent larger for the same grain and sharpness. It became a favourite for modern T-grain films and for scanning. Its one well-known weakness was sudden failure: an exhausted or contaminated batch could stop developing without warning, so careful users tested each batch."
        "stage/develop":
            return "The developer is a reducing agent. It turns exposed grains, those carrying a latent image, into metallic silver, and leaves unexposed grains alone, at least for a while. Given enough time, it starts on them too, which shows as fog.\n\nHow far development goes depends on time, temperature and agitation. Longer, warmer or more agitated development produces more density, mostly in the highlights, so contrast rises. It also lets weakly exposed grains develop, which effectively raises the film's speed. That is a push: rate the film faster, underexpose, and develop longer. A pull does the opposite.\n\nIn color development, the developer that has given up its electrons reacts with the couplers next to the grain and forms dye. The silver is later bleached away, leaving only the dye clouds, which is why color grain looks soft and round.\n\nHere, time and temperature start at the process's own normal, from the manufacturer's tables."
        "stage/easel":
            return "In the darkroom, the easel holds the paper under the enlarger and its blades frame the picture. Framing is the last decision, and the photographer's alone.\n\nHere, the easel crops the frame, corrects perspective and rotation (the guides find the lines that should be vertical or horizontal), and darkens or lightens the edges, as printers burned in the corners of a print with their hands or a card to hold the eye in the frame.\n\nBecause it comes last, the easel works on the finished picture. Cropping here does not change how the film was exposed or how big its grain is: the grain belongs to the negative and its format, chosen in the Film node."
        "stage/exposure":
            return "Exposure is light times time: how bright the image on the film plane is, and for how long the shutter lets it fall. Photographers count it in stops. One stop is a doubling or a halving.\n\nNegative film is forgiving. A color negative records about ten stops or more, and two stops of overexposure often look better than a normal exposure, with denser, cleaner shadows. Underexposure is what hurts: shadow detail never reaches the film, and the grain of a thin negative shows.\n\nSlide film is the opposite. Its range is about five or six stops, and highlights burn out with little warning. Slide shooters often underexposed by a third of a stop to keep color deep.\n\nHere, Exposure changes the light reaching the film, and nothing later takes it back. Underexpose and then push in the Develop node, and you see what a pushed negative really looks like."
        "stage/file":
            return "A raw file holds what each photosite of the sensor counted: photons, nearly in proportion to the light that fell on it. Nothing has been decided yet. There is no white balance, no contrast curve and no color rendering, only a record of the scene's light behind a mosaic of red, green and blue filters.\n\nThat is why the recipe starts here. Developing the raw recovers the scene's light as numbers that still add up the way light does: twice the light is twice the value. Everything after this node treats those numbers as light falling on a lens and then on film, just as the real scene once did.\n\nA JPEG is different. It has already been through a camera's own rendering, so it is turned back into light first (the inverse of a display transform), which works well for textures and screen content but cannot bring back what the camera threw away."
        "stage/film":
            return "Film is a thin plastic base coated with gelatin holding crystals of silver halide, the grains. A photon absorbed by a grain can leave a few atoms of metallic silver, a latent image too small to see. Bigger grains catch light more easily, so fast films have bigger grains and show more grain.\n\nColor film stacks three such layers, made sensitive by dyes to blue, green and red light, each holding a coupler that will become a dye: yellow, magenta and cyan. Between and within the layers, chemistry links them. Development in one layer releases inhibitors that hold back its neighbors, the interimage effect, which makes film colors richer than a simple mix.\n\nLight also scatters inside the emulsion and some bounces back off the base: halation, the warm glow around bright lights. Here each stock is built from its manufacturer's published data: the layers' spectral sensitivities, characteristic curves, dyes, sharpness and granularity."
        "stage/filter":
            return "A filter changes the light before the film sees it. Two families matter here.\n\nConversion filters fix a mismatch between the light and the film. The orange 85 turns daylight into something close to the 3200 K that tungsten stock expects, and costs two thirds of a stop. Without it, tungsten film outdoors goes blue.\n\nBlack-and-white photographers use strong color filters to change which colors become light or dark grey. A yellow 8 darkens a blue sky a little, as the eye expects. An orange 21 darkens it more and lightens skin. A red 25 turns the sky almost black and makes clouds dramatic; Ansel Adams made Monolith, the Face of Half Dome, with a deep red filter. A green 11 lightens foliage. A blue 47 does the opposite of the red.\n\nEvery filter takes away light. The filter factor is how much more exposure it needs, and this node opens up by that factor, as the photographer would."
        "stage/grade":
            return "Once the negative is scanned, it is images again, and a colorist shapes them. On film-originated television this happened on the telecine itself, with controllers such as the da Vinci, adjusting lift, gamma and gain per color and per scene.\n\nThe tools here are the familiar ones: tone (contrast, highlights, shadows, whites and blacks), levels, curves, vibrance and saturation, a mixer of eight hue bands, color wheels for shadows, midtones and highlights, and a black-and-white conversion with its own channel weights.\n\nA grade cannot add what the film did not record. It can, however, push a look much further than any lab could, which is why this stage sits after the scan: the film decides the material, the grade decides the picture."
        "stage/lens":
            return "Every lens lets less light reach the corners of the frame than the center. Part of it is geometry: light reaching the edge arrives at an angle, spread over more area and seen through a narrower opening, which by itself dims it by about the fourth power of the cosine of the angle. Part of it is the lens barrel cutting off oblique light, worst at wide apertures.\n\nCinematographers lived with it and often liked it: the gentle darkening draws the eye to the middle of the frame. Modern raw developers usually correct it away. Here it happens to the light before the film sees it, so the darker corners also get the film's response there, a slightly thinner negative and a touch more grain, rather than a vignette painted on afterwards."
        "stage/lightbox":
            return "A slide is the picture itself. Reversal film comes out of processing as a positive, so there is nothing to print or invert: it is viewed by light shining through it, on a light table with a loupe, or projected.\n\nLight tables for judging color are made to a standard, D50 light, close to daylight at 5000 K, so that everyone in a print shop or an agency saw the same slide the same way. Seen like this, a slide shows its full range: the deepest dye densities of a good slide reach beyond 3.5, far more than a print on paper can hold.\n\nHere, the slide is lit by D50 light and the light coming through its dyes is what is shown."
        "stage/print":
            return "In the cinema chain, the camera negative was printed onto print film: a fine-grained, high-contrast stock exposed through the negative by a printer, then developed. The print is a positive, meant to be projected.\n\nThe lab controlled color with printer lights: the red, green and blue intensities of the printer lamp, in steps called points. A timer chose them shot by shot, which is where the term color timing comes from. Raising a light adds density in that color's dye on the print.\n\nPrint film is steep: its contrast multiplies the negative's, and its dyes give the deep blacks and saturated, slightly crushed color of a film print. Kodak Vision Color Print Film 2383 was the release print stock of most films from the late 1990s on, and it is the print stock here."
        "stage/project":
            return "A film print is made to be seen by projection. A xenon arc lamp, close to daylight in color, shines through the print and a lens throws the image onto a screen many meters away.\n\nThe dark theater matters. With no other light, the eye adapts to the screen, and the print's dense blacks look truly black. Some light always strays, from the lens, the screen and the room, and lifts the deepest shadows a little: projection flare.\n\nProjected film has a look of its own: rich, saturated midtones, highlights that roll off rather than clip, and shadows that sink. This node shows the print as it would look on such a screen."
        "stage/scan":
            return "A negative is not a picture yet. It is inverted, and color negatives carry an orange mask, extra dye that corrects for the cyan and magenta dyes absorbing light they should not. To see the image, the film is read as densities and turned back into light.\n\nFor television, that was the telecine: a Rank Cintel flying-spot scanner, and later the Spirit DataCine, reading the negative straight to video. A colorist set the transfer per scene: how warm, how dense, where black sits. Modern film scanners read printing density, the densities print film would see, so a scan can stand in for a print.\n\nThe scan here measures the negative through the densities its dyes produce and maps them back to scene light, set up once for a normally exposed and developed negative. Like a telecine, it is calibrated on a reference negative, Kodak's Vision3 250D, so each stock shows its own color: the softer palette of 1990s EXR, the saturation of Vision3 50D or Ektar. Calibration set to Matched fits the scanner to the stock instead, returning colors to themselves. Timing (Warmth and Tint), Contrast and Black adjust it as the telecine operator would."
        "stage/view":
            return "A display cannot show the light a scan holds. Scene light ranges over many stops; a screen has a fixed white and a limited range. A view transform decides how one maps to the other, rolling off highlights, keeping midtones and choosing how saturated colors are shown.\n\nACES 2.0 is the Academy Color Encoding System's current output transform, used across the film industry, with highlights that desaturate gracefully as they approach white. Standard is a plainer filmic curve. Linear shows the values as they are, clipped.\n\nIn the cinema chain this node is replaced by Print and Project, which are their own view transform: the print film's curve and the theater."
        "stage/white_balance":
            return "Light has a color. A tungsten lamp burns at about 3200 K and gives warm, orange light; daylight is near 5500 to 6500 K and bluer. Our eyes adapt and see a white shirt as white under both. Film does not adapt: it is made for one kind of light.\n\nMotion picture stocks come in two balances. Tungsten (T) stocks such as Vision3 500T expect 3200 K studio lamps; daylight (D) stocks expect the sun. Shooting tungsten film outdoors gives a strong blue cast unless an orange 85 filter is put on the lens, at the cost of two thirds of a stop.\n\nHere, Temperature and Tint describe the light the scene was lit by. Left as shot, they are the camera's own estimate. With the Filter node's Balance set to Matched, the light is balanced to the stock, as a lab would; set to None, the film sees the light as it was, color cast and all."
        "stock/eastman-color-negative-5251":
            return "Eastman Color Negative 5251 was Kodak's tungsten camera negative of the 1960s, introduced in 1962 to replace 5250 with finer grain and better color. It was rated EI 50 at 3200 K and was developed in the original ECN process (later called ECN-1): 12 minutes in SD-30 at 21 °C, with a ferricyanide bleach. Prints were made on Eastman Color Print 5385.\n\n2001: A Space Odyssey was photographed in Super Panavision 70, on 65 mm negative with spherical lenses, and released in 70 mm prints from April 1968. The common technical listings name the camera stock as Eastman 5251. That fits the dates: its faster successor, 5254, was only presented to the SMPTE in May 1968, after the premiere, so 5251 is very likely, though no Kodak or production document we have names it.\n\nNo data sheet for 5251 survives online. The curves here come from Kodak's 1968 SMPTE paper on 5254, which states that the new film has the same structure, the same spectral sensitivities and the same dyes as 5251, and is one stop faster; the curves are moved one stop to account for that. The paper's exposure axis carries no numbers, so where normal exposure falls is an estimate, and it publishes no grain or sharpness data."
        "stock/eastman-color-negative-5254":
            return "Kodak presented 5254 to the SMPTE in May 1968 as its new camera negative: the same structure, spectral sensitivities and dyes as 5251, and one stop faster, rated 100 under tungsten light. It was developed in ECN-1, and it carried American features into the early 1970s, until ECN-2 and 5247 replaced it.\n\nIt is the negative of much of the New Hollywood. Gordon Willis rated it at 250 and pushed it a stop for The Godfather, and Stanley Kubrick and John Alcott pushed the whole of Barry Lyndon a stop on it, its candlelit scenes lit by candles alone through a Zeiss f/0.7 lens.\n\nNo data sheet for 5254 survives online. Since Kodak's own paper says it is 5251 a stop faster, it is built here from 5251's curves moved one stop; its grain, coarser for the speed, is an estimate."
        "stock/eastman-double-x-5222":
            return "Double-X is Kodak's black-and-white camera negative for motion pictures, usually dated to 1959 and still made today in 35 mm (5222) and 16 mm (7222). It is a fast panchromatic film, rated EI 250 in daylight and 200 under tungsten for development to a gamma of 0.65.\n\nLaboratories develop it in D-96, a developer made for motion picture black-and-white work, and the development time sets the contrast. Kodak's 2022 sheet shows the range: 4 minutes gives a gamma of 0.50 and 12 minutes 1.05, with 6½ minutes landing at the recommended 0.66. Its diffuse rms granularity is 14, and its resolving power is 100 lines per millimetre at high contrast.\n\nStill photographers know it as CineStill bwXX, which is Double-X respooled for 35 mm cameras."
        "stock/fujifilm-neopan-100-acros-ii":
            return "Acros was Fujifilm's fine-grained ISO 100 black-and-white film, known for some of the finest grain and best sharpness of any conventional black-and-white film, and for remarkable reciprocity: it needs no exposure correction for exposures up to two minutes, which made it a favourite for night photography and long exposures.\n\nFujifilm stopped making it in 2018, and Acros II appeared in 2019, made with a new emulsion technology Fujifilm calls P.I.D.C. Its data sheet gives characteristic curves for D-76 at 4, 7 and 10 minutes, which this simulator uses to model how development time changes contrast and speed, and a granularity of 7 in Microfine developer.\n\nAcros' grain is a fine, tight texture, which is why Fujifilm's digital cameras imitate it with a grain of their own. Here its grain comes from small, crisp clouds of silver on a single layer."
        "stock/fujifilm-pro-400h":
            return "Pro 400H was Fujifilm's professional ISO 400 daylight color negative, processed in C-41 (Fujifilm's CN-16). Fujifilm's sheet describes it as reproducing neutral greys faithfully from under- to overexposure and giving superb skin tones with smooth gradation from highlights to shadows, which made it a portrait and wedding film. Fujifilm discontinued it in 2021.\n\nIts distinctive feature was a fourth color-sensitive layer, in addition to the usual blue, green and red, meant to make the film see colors more as the eye does. The sheet draws it as a \"cyan sensitive layer\" peaking near 520 nm, about 0.9 log units below the green layer's peak. It forms no image of its own: through interlayer effects it adjusts the red layer's response to blue-green light.\n\nThe sheet's curves are vectors and are read here directly, the fourth layer included. Fujifilm gives a diffuse rms granularity of 4, but on its own negative-film scale, which it says cannot be compared with slide films; the grain strength used here is an estimate matched to Kodak Portra 400."
        "stock/fujifilm-velvia-100":
            return "Fujifilm introduced Velvia 100 in 2005, when the original Velvia was withdrawn, as an ISO 100 film with Velvia's saturation. Fujifilm called its color saturation the world's highest, and gave it a diffuse rms granularity of 8, finer than Velvia 50's 9. It uses a color-extension layer adjusted from Velvia 100F's to render sunrise and sunset hues. Velvia 50 returned in 2007, and the two were sold side by side.\n\nLike Velvia 50 it is a reversal film, developed in E-6 into a slide, with deep blacks, saturated greens and reds and little room for overexposure. Its resolving power is 160 lines/mm at high contrast, among the highest of any slide film. Fujifilm withdrew it from the United States in 2021 over a trace chemical restricted by the EPA.\n\nIts sheet's graphs are vector drawings, read here directly: Status A characteristic curves that fall from a maximum density of about 3.8, the three layers' spectral sensitivities, the yellow, magenta and cyan dyes each normalized to a peak of 1.0, and one MTF curve."
        "stock/fujifilm-velvia-50":
            return "When Fujifilm introduced Velvia in 1990, it changed landscape and nature photography. Its colors are far more saturated than the slide films before it, especially greens and reds, with deep blacks and very fine grain. It was rated ISO 50, and many photographers rated it at 40.\n\nVelvia is a reversal film: it is developed in E-6 into a slide, a positive to be seen on a light table or projected. It has a narrow exposure range, about five stops, and little forgiveness for overexposure, so landscape photographers metered carefully and shot in soft light, often with graduated filters to hold the sky.\n\nThe original was discontinued in 2005 because some of its materials became unavailable, and Velvia 50 returned in 2007 in a new formulation made to look the same. The curves here come from that film's data sheet."
        "stock/ilford-hp5-plus":
            return "HP5 Plus is Ilford's fast black-and-white film, rated ISO 400. Its line goes back to Ilford's HP (hypersensitive panchromatic) films of the 1930s; HP5 appeared in 1976 and the current HP5 Plus in 1989. Like Tri-X it uses conventional, cubic grain, and the two have long been the standard fast black-and-white films.\n\nIlford's sheet recommends it at EI 400 but says it produces good prints at up to EI 3200 with extended development, and notes that its exposure range is based on practical tests, not on the foot speed of the ISO standard. It is known for wide latitude and an easy, forgiving tonal range.\n\nIlford publishes its graphs as images, with one characteristic curve, in ILFOTEC HC for 6 1/2 minutes at 20 C, on a relative exposure scale, and a wedge spectrogram to tungsten light reaching to about 655 nm. Both are traced here. Ilford gives no MTF or rms granularity, so this stock's sharpness and grain are less certain than those of Kodak's films."
        "stock/kodak-ektachrome-e100":
            return "Kodak stopped making its Ektachrome slide films in 2012. Kodak Alaris announced their return in January 2017, and Ektachrome E100 arrived in 2018: a new daylight slide film for the E-6 process, rated ISO 100.\n\nThe sheet describes it plainly: extremely fine grain (diffuse rms 8), a low D-min for clean whites, moderately enhanced saturation with a neutral balance, and a low-contrast tone scale. It is a T-Grain emulsion and needs no correction for exposures from 1/10,000 s to 10 s. Kodak matched its three color records so that the grey scale stays neutral from highlights into shadows.\n\nAs a reversal film it is developed into a positive, and its characteristic curves fall as exposure rises, from a maximum density near 3.7 down to the clear base. Kodak's own advice is that a slide film has much less exposure range than a negative, about half a stop either way for the best result, so it rewards careful metering."
        "stock/kodak-ektar-100":
            return "Kodak introduced Ektar 100 in 2008, reviving a name it had used for a line of fine-grained films in the late 1980s. It is a daylight color negative for Process C-41, and Kodak's sheet calls it the finest-grained color negative film available, with high saturation and \"ultra-vivid\" color. It is built with the same Vision motion picture technology as the current Portra films, so it shares their dye set.\n\nEktar is known for strong, clean color and more contrast than Portra. Kodak recommends it for nature, travel and outdoor photography, and for fashion and product work. Kodak gives no rms granularity for it, only a Print Grain Index: below 25, the threshold of visible grain, for a 4x6 print from 35 mm, and 38 at 8x10.\n\nThe sheet's curves are drawn as vectors, and the simulator reads them directly: characteristic curves with normal exposure at log H -0.84, the three layers' spectral sensitivities, the midscale neutral and D-min dye densities, and MTF curves that stay near 100 percent to about 20 cycles/mm. Its grain strength here is an estimate scaled from the Print Grain Index."
        "stock/kodak-exr-200t-5293":
            return "The medium-speed tungsten stock of the EXR line, rated EI 200 at 3200 K. With less speed it has finer grain than 500T, and it was a workhorse of studio productions in the early and mid 1990s, both for features and for television.\n\nDirectors of photography on television valued its slightly higher contrast: material that is going to be transferred, compressed and broadcast tends to lose snap along the way, so a negative that starts a little punchier survives better. The DP of Spin City chose it for exactly that reason.\n\nThe data sheet for 5293 also publishes the spectral densities of its cyan, magenta and yellow dyes. They are the dyes of the ECN-2 process, which this simulator uses for its EXR stocks."
        "stock/kodak-exr-500t-5298":
            return "EXR was Kodak's motion picture negative line of the late 1980s and early 1990s. Its 500T was the fast, tungsten-balanced member, rated EI 500 under 3200 K studio light and EI 320 in daylight behind an 85 filter. Kodak's 1993 data sheet for 5298 is the source of the curves used here.\n\nIt is a stock of its time. Its red layer is noticeably softer than its green and blue, its blue layer the grainiest, and it carries a rem-jet backing, a black layer on the back that stops light reflecting off the base, so it shows little halation.\n\nFast tungsten negative like this was the natural choice for 1990s American television shot on 35 mm, such as Friends: sets lit by tungsten lamps, a negative telecined straight to video, never printed. No source names the stock Friends used in each season; 5298 and its slower sibling 5293 are the likely candidates for the early years."
        "stock/kodak-portra-160":
            return "Portra arrived in 1998 as the successor to Kodak's Vericolor professional films, first in NC (natural color) and VC (vivid color) versions. In 2011 the two 160-speed films were replaced by a single Portra 160, made with Kodak's Vision motion picture film technology, the same step the new Portra 400 had taken a year earlier.\n\nKodak describes it as having finer grain for scanning and enlargement while keeping the \"smooth and natural skin tone reproduction\" the family is known for, and recommends it for portrait, fashion and commercial work in the studio or on location. Its contrast is low and its color restrained, so it renders faces and pastel light gently and holds detail in bright highlights.\n\nThe sheet gives normal exposure at log H -1.051 and a Print Grain Index of 28 for a 4x6 print from 35 mm, finer than Portra 400's 37. Its curves, sensitivities, dye densities and MTF are read from the sheet's vector graphs. Kodak publishes no rms granularity for it, so the grain strength used here is an estimate scaled from the Print Grain Index."
        "stock/kodak-portra-400":
            return "Portra arrived in 1998 as the successor to Kodak's Vericolor professional films, first in NC (natural color) and VC (vivid color) versions. In late 2010 a single new Portra 400 replaced them, and Kodak said it was built with technology from its Vision motion picture films, aiming at natural skin tones and fine grain for its speed. It became the color negative of wedding, portrait and fashion photographers, and later of a new generation shooting film for its look.\n\nLike all color negatives, it has wide latitude: it holds detail when overexposed by several stops, and many photographers rate it at 200 or lower for denser, cleaner negatives and softer pastel color. Its sheet calls it the world's finest-grain high-speed color negative.\n\nKodak's still sheets draw the midscale neutral but not each dye, so the dye shapes here are Vision3 250D's, the motion picture film Portra's technology comes from. Its characteristic curves, spectral sensitivities and MTF are read from the sheet itself; Kodak gives a print grain index rather than an rms granularity, so its grain is an estimate between Vision3 250D's and 500T's."
        "stock/kodak-portra-800":
            return "Portra 800 is the fastest film in Kodak's Portra line of professional color negatives for Process C-41. Kodak's sheet describes it as having well balanced saturation, very fine grain for its speed and \"best-in-class underexposure latitude\", and recommends it for long lenses and low light.\n\nIt is the only current Kodak still film whose sheet draws push-processed curves: besides normal processing at EI 800, it shows the film exposed at EI 1600 and given a one-stop push, and at EI 3200 with a two-stop push. Pushing raises contrast and density across the scale; at the same exposure the green layer reaches about 1.55 after one stop of push and 1.7 after two, against 1.4 normally. The simulator keeps all three sets of curves.\n\nIts Print Grain Index is 48 for a 4x6 print from 35 mm, against Portra 400's 37. Kodak gives no rms granularity, so the grain strength here is an estimate scaled from that index. The sheet puts normal exposure at log H -1.74."
        "stock/kodak-t-max-100":
            return "Kodak introduced T-MAX 100 and T-MAX 400 in 1986, the first black-and-white films built on its tabular T-Grain emulsions. Flat silver crystals catch more light for their volume than cubic ones, so the film reaches a given speed with finer grain. Kodak's sheet describes it as having extremely high sharpness, extremely fine grain and very high resolving power, and suggests it for detailed subjects, copying and photomicrography.\n\nIt is a cleaner, more clinical film than Tri-X, with a long straight curve and smooth tones. Its developer and time matter more than with traditional films, and Kodak warns against developing it by inspection.\n\nThe sheet gives a diffuse rms granularity of 8 in D-76 and resolving power of 63 lines/mm at low contrast and 200 at high contrast. Its characteristic curves cover D-76 at 6, 7.5 and 10 minutes, T-MAX RS at four times and T-MAX Developer at four times, and its MTF stays near 100 percent to about 50 cycles/mm. All of these are read from the sheet's vector graphs."
        "stock/kodak-tri-x-400":
            return "Tri-X is one of the longest-lived films still made. Kodak introduced it in 1940 as a sheet film and brought it to 35 mm and 120 in 1954. Its current version, 400TX, is a high-speed panchromatic black-and-white negative with conventional, cubic grain, and Kodak recommends it for dim light, fast action and push processing.\n\nIts look is the classic one of documentary and street photography: visible, crisp grain, strong midtone contrast and a shoulder that holds bright skies. It responds strongly to developer and time, which is why photographers have pushed it to EI 1600 and 3200 for decades.\n\nKodak's sheet draws characteristic curves for D-76 at 6, 8, 10 and 12 minutes and for T-MAX Developer at 6, 7, 9 and 11 minutes, each for 35 mm and 120. The simulator uses them to model how development changes contrast. It also gives a spectral sensitivity reaching to about 650 nm, an MTF curve, and a diffuse rms granularity of 17, about twice T-MAX 100's."
        "stock/kodak-vision-320t-5277":
            return "Vision 320T was made for television. Kodak designed it with a lower contrast than its feature stocks, because a telecine transfer and a television screen both add contrast of their own; starting flatter keeps detail in the faces and in the shadows of a set.\n\nIts 320 speed sat between the 200 and 500 stocks, fast enough for a sitcom's lighting with good depth of field across a stage. Shows such as Everybody Loves Raymond used it. Its look is clean, slightly soft in contrast, and it takes a grade well."
        "stock/kodak-vision-500t-5279":
            return "In 1996 Kodak began replacing EXR with Vision, and 5279 was the first: a 500-speed tungsten negative with finer grain than the EXR 500T it succeeded. Its sheet places a normal grey exposure about four stops above the film's threshold, which shows how much shadow latitude a negative carries below a correctly exposed face.\n\nVision 500T became one of the most used stocks of the late 1990s and early 2000s, in features, commercials and television. It was followed by Vision2 500T 5218 in 2002 and Vision3 500T 5219 in 2007, still in production today. Each generation kept the look recognizably Kodak, warm and smooth, with less grain and more highlight latitude."
        "stock/kodak-vision3-200t-5213":
            return "Vision3 200T came out in 2010 as the medium-speed tungsten stock of the family. Kodak described it as a 200-speed film with the image structure of a 100-speed one, two films in one. It is rated EI 200 at 3200 K and 125 in daylight with an 85 filter, and it is processed in ECN-2.\n\nIt shares the Vision3 traits: extended highlight latitude, reduced grain in the shadows, and a look matched to the other stocks, so that scenes shot on 200T cut with scenes shot on 500T or 250D. Kodak recommends it both for controlled studio interiors and for high-contrast exteriors.\n\nIts sheet marks normal exposure at log H -1.284, the centre of a sixteen-stop scale; there the green record reads about 1.35 in Status M density, close to where the other Vision3 negatives place a grey card."
        "stock/kodak-vision3-250d-5207":
            return "Vision3 250D is the medium-speed daylight stock of the family, introduced in 2009. It is rated EI 250 at 5500 K and 64 under tungsten with an 80A filter, and it is developed in ECN-2 like the other Vision3 negatives.\n\nKodak describes it as Vision3 technology in a daylight emulsion: the look of Vision2, with less grain in the shadows and more than two stops of extra highlight latitude. The sheet singles out the red and green records, whose grain improves most when the film is underexposed, so dark parts of a scan come out cleaner.\n\nFor an exterior in sun, 250D gives enough speed to stop down for depth of field while keeping the grain of a medium-speed film. Its MTF curves are among the best of the Vision3 negatives, holding above 40 percent at 70 cycles per millimetre in the blue and green records."
        "stock/kodak-vision3-500t-5219":
            return "Vision3 500T was the first of the Vision3 family, released in November 2007, and it is still in production. It is a 500-speed tungsten negative for the ECN-2 process, rated EI 500 at 3200 K and 320 in daylight behind an 85 filter. Kodak kept the overall look of Vision2 and changed the two ends of the curve: dye layering technology lowers the grain in the shadows, and what Kodak calls sub-micron imaging sensors give the highlights more room. The sheet says about two extra stops of highlight detail can be recovered when the film is scanned.\n\nIts characteristic curves span sixteen stops on Kodak's graph and are still climbing at the bright end, which is why Kodak widened the graph's exposure scale for Vision3. Normal exposure sits halfway along that scale, eight stops from either end of the graph.\n\nVision3 500T is also the film inside CineStill 800T, which is sold with the rem-jet backing removed so that it can be developed in C-41. Without that backing, light scattered back from the base gives bright lights their red halo."
        "stock/kodak-vision3-50d-5203":
            return "The slowest Vision3 negative, 50D was added in 2011 and Kodak said its grain was the world's finest. It is rated EI 50 in daylight and 12 under tungsten with an 80A filter, and it is processed in ECN-2.\n\nKodak aimed it at bright, high-contrast daylight exteriors, where its long exposure range holds both a sunlit sky and a face in shade, and at film-out work, where an image recorded from digital files needs the finest grain available. Its granularity curves are the lowest of the family: in the red and green records the rms granularity stays near or below 0.006 across the whole scale.\n\nLike 500T, it has a respooled cousin for still cameras: CineStill 50D is 5203 with the rem-jet backing removed so that it can be developed in C-41."
        _:
            return ""
